local

# Build artifacts
build/

# Root-level compiled binaries. `go build ./cmd/x` and `go build ./samples/x`
# drop their output here, and four of them were committed by accident. That is
# not merely untidy: publish-oss.sh rsyncs the WORKING TREE, so anything tracked
# at root ships to the public repo verbatim. Three of these were live on
# AI-AgentLens/agentshield-oss for months with 19 embedded build paths of the
# form /Users/<handle>/dev/<old-private-repo-name>/... — the privacy scan in
# publish-oss.sh reads text files and cannot see inside a Mach-O.
# Build to ./build/ or use `go run`; never commit a binary at root.
/cli-demo
/mcp-demo
/server
/gen-premium-manifest

# Auto-generated reports (these are generated by tests / tooling)
FAILING_TESTS.md
# mcp-gen's TN work queue for Baby Kai (cmd/mcp-gen/main.go). Generator output,
# never committed in this repo's history — but it was not ignored either, and
# that is the part that matters: publish-oss.sh builds the public tree by
# rsyncing the WORKING tree and then `git add -A`, so .gitignore is what decides
# whether a generated file ships. build/ and compliance-bin/ are filtered by the
# entries above (verified: they sit in the temp tree and are never committed);
# this one was not, so an internal work queue would have gone public on the next
# publish. Ignoring it is the fix — an EXCLUDES entry would work too, but the
# ignore also keeps `make mcp-gen` from leaving the tree dirty.
packs/community/mcp/tn-pool.json
internal/guardian/testdata/GUARDIAN_REDTEAM_REPORT.md
internal/analyzer/testdata/PIPELINE_REDTEAM_REPORT.md
internal/mcp/testdata/MCP_REDTEAM_REPORT.md
compliance-bin/

# Local /ultrareview worktree clones (per-agent isolation, never commit)
.claude/worktrees/
